# ── Your agents. None of this belongs in a repository. ───────────────────
#
# This repo has no data directory, which makes it feel safer than it is. The
# way it leaks is not a stray database — it's your real configuration, your
# real transcripts, and your real conversations ending up in a config file, a
# fixture, or a comment. `tests/test_no_private_content.py` is the other half
# of this file; neither one is enough alone.
#
# Every comment here is on its own line, and has to stay that way: git only
# treats `#` as a comment at the START of a line. `vitals.json  # yours` is a
# pattern matching a file literally called "vitals.json  # yours", i.e.
# nothing — which looks exactly like a rule that works.

# Your live config: agent names, home directories, and every word you inject
# into their context. `vitals.example.json` is the one that gets committed.
vitals.json

# Anything else you keep next to it
vitals.*.json
!vitals.example.json

# What the hook writes down. Contains session ids, working directories, and
# which channel each wake-up came from.
*.log
logs/

# The words that would identify you, for tests/test_no_private_content.py.
# Committing this file would publish exactly the list it exists to keep out.
.private-terms.txt

# Transcripts and pane captures. If you copy one here to debug against, this
# is the line that stops you publishing it.
*.jsonl
transcripts/
captures/
scratch/

# ── Junk ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
__pycache__/
*.pyc
